Important Note

Incomplete submissions are expected and completely fine.
All four topics below might be new to you. They will probably feel difficult when you
first read them, and that is normal. Please do not let that stop you from picking one.
Start simple. Figure out what the topic actually is before you write any code. Read the
paper. Read other people’s implementations, because all of this is published work and the
reference code is public. Use whatever helps you learn, including AI assistants.
We scoped these tasks so that finishing everything in two weeks is hard. We are not
checking whether you finished. We are looking for signs that you understood what you
were building. Even if you manage to complete just 2 out of 6 points, that will be more
than enough for us.
Whatever you manage to build, you will come out of this knowing a lot more than when
you started.





Common Requirements
Every submission must include:
1. A working repository: Code that runs, with a README.md explaining how to run it.
2. A correctness harness: An actual test script that compares your implementation against
a trusted reference and clearly reports pass or fail.
3. A write-up: Submit a 2–4 page WRITEUP.md.
4. A commit history: We want to see how the work developed. Make regular and meaningful
commits rather than submitting the entire project in one commit.






Task 1: Neural Network with Manual Backpropagation
Build a small artificial neural network and implement backpropagation yourself. You may use
NumPy, but you may not use autograd or .backward() for your implementation.
Deliverables
Item Required deliverable
1.1 Build a small feedforward neural network using basic matrix operations.
1.2 Implement the forward pass using linear layers and an activation function of your choice.
1.3 Implement the backward pass manually using the chain rule. Derive the gradients for every
layer and include the mathematics in your write-up or code comments.
1.4 Check your gradients against torch.autograd or numerical gradient checking. Your results
should match within a reasonable tolerance.

1.5 Train the network on a small real task, such as a reduced MNIST dataset or a simple classifi-
cation or regression dataset. Show that the loss decreases during training.

1.6 In your write-up, discuss any gradient mistakes you made and explain how you identified and
fixed them.


Stretch goal. Add another activation–loss combination and derive its gradients. You may also
implement an optimizer such as Momentum or Adam.
